description:
Send blocks larger than one packet as fragments, and put them back together safely on the other side (lesson 5.05).

```cpp
struct Fragment
{
    std::uint16_t block;
    std::uint8_t index;
    std::uint8_t count;
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> data;
};

std::vector<Fragment> split(std::uint16_t block, const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& data, std::size_t maxFragment);

class Reassembler
{
public:
    explicit Reassembler(double timeout);
    std::optional<std::vector<std::uint8_t>> add(const Fragment& f, double now);
    void expire(double now);
    std::size_t pending() const;
};
```

**split** cuts `data` into pieces of `maxFragment` bytes (the last may be shorter), numbered from 0, each knowing the total count. Empty data is one empty fragment. If `maxFragment` is 0 or more than 255 fragments would be needed, it returns no fragments.

**Reassembler** keeps the blocks in progress, keyed by block id. `add` first calls `expire(now)`, then:

- drops the fragment (returning nothing and changing nothing) if its count is 0, its index is not below its count, its data is longer than 1200 bytes, or a block with the same id is in progress with a **different** count;
- ignores a fragment whose index has already arrived for that block;
- starts a new block if none is in progress with that id, unless 16 blocks are already in progress, in which case the fragment is dropped; a block's timer starts with its first fragment;
- when a block has all its fragments, removes it and returns its data in index order.

`expire(now)` removes every block whose first fragment arrived more than `timeout` before `now`. `pending` is the number of blocks in progress.

